e or Sentry, sampled, on the queue, never inline on the hot path. Alternatives and why they are weaker: (a) policy functions called from a view or trigger: shadow evaluation would need a parallel copy of every policy, which is a second source of truth that drifts; (b) pg_audit logs statements, not row-level would-be denials, so it cannot tell you what RLS would have hidden; (c) EXPLAIN shows the policy quals but not the result difference, useful only for planner-cost review. A trick for single-connection shadowing: policies written as USING (shadow_check(row) OR NOT enforce_flag()) where shadow_check also RAISE LOGs on false, so the policy never blocks but logs; this works for reads but costs per-row function calls on large tables and breaks index use, so keep it as a variation, not the base. Caveat: set-diff catches rows RLS hides but not rows PHP hides that RLS allows, so run the diff both directions and treat the latter as an informational finding, since RLS is the coarser net.\",\"identity_plumbing\":\"Per request, wrap in DB::transaction and call select set_config('app.current_user_id', ?, true) as the first statement (parameterized, is_local=true, same as llame's tenant-db.service.ts); reset to '' on exit as llame does. Hazards: (1) pgbouncer transaction pooling is safe only because the setting is transaction-local; session-mode SET or SET without LOCAL leaks identity across clients, so ban non-local sets by lint; (2) Laravel does not wrap requests in a transaction by default, so every query outside a transaction sees no identity and fails closed, which means a request-scoped transaction or a connection decorator is required and changes behavior of every endpoint that today relies on autocommit (long requests holding transactions open, and idle-in-transaction timeouts on RabbitMQ or SSH calls inside request handlers); (3) queue workers, scheduler, artisan, and the RabbitMQ task consumers have no user: they need an explicit system identity (separate role or app